// src/views.mjs — server-rendered HTML. No build step, no framework, no client-side rendering.
//
// Visual system: `design-system/gemba/MASTER.md` — generated by ui-ux-pro-max, held to the warm
// paper + orange palette this product asked for. Site structure follows arbolus.com as the layout
// reference: a thin dark utility bar, a light nav bar with the logo left and one filled CTA right,
// a centred hero (eyebrow stat row → very large headline → lead → one button), a dark full-width
// band carrying profile cards, alternating light sections with card grids, a quote-card row, a
// resource-card grid, a closing dark CTA band, and a four-column footer.
//
// Four deliberate departures from the generated system, each with its reason:
//
//   1. No webfont request. The reference leans on a geometric sans (Geist); this runs as an
//      appliance on a factory LAN and behind a tunnel, so a fonts.googleapis.com call is a
//      third-party request on every page load and a blank flash while it resolves. The stack
//      prefers the system UI font and adopts the reference's *scale and tracking* instead.
//   2. No animation library, and one script in the whole product. Pages are built on the server and
//      stay readable with JavaScript off; the single inline script only reports that a slow submit is
//      in flight (see `data-pending` below). No client-side rendering, no hydration, no fetch. The one
//      entry motion is CSS and it is off under `prefers-reduced-motion`.
//   3. The refusal is graphite, not red and not brand orange: it is an answer, not a failure, and
//      it must not be mistaken for the brand's own accent. The escalation beside it is the CTA.
//   4. No proof strip and no logo carousel. There are no customers yet, and the README promises
//      not to fake evidence.

